{%- comment -%}
  Catalogue card — port of components/shop/product-card.tsx.

  A square crop of the garment with the flag badge at the top left and two
  actions at the bottom right, then the material line in camel, the name in
  Playfair, and the price.

  The card is one stretched link — the name's anchor covers the whole card — so
  there is one tab stop per product and no nested interactive elements. The two
  actions sit above that overlay and are their own stops.

  The bag action goes to the product page rather than adding straight to the bag.
  Every piece has five sizes and five colourways, so a one-click add would have
  to guess a variant; for apparel that is a return, not a conversion. It is
  labelled as what it does.

  The name drops one rung on the system's own scale — H3 20 instead of the card's
  22 — below `sm`, where the catalogue runs two cards to a row and the column is
  about 155px wide. A 22px serif wraps to three lines there.

  The hover/focus scale was a Motion client component in React because the card
  is one stretched link and pointer and keyboard land on different elements.
  In CSS that is `group-hover` plus `group-has-focus-visible`, which covers both
  for free and costs no JavaScript — so the whole card is now server-rendered.

  `group-focus-within` was the first spelling and it was wrong on a phone: the
  card contains a save `<button>`, so tapping it focuses it, `:focus-within`
  matches, and the photograph sits zoomed with no pointer that can ever leave.
  `:focus-visible` is what the keyboard path actually needs, and a touch tap
  does not set it.

  VIEW TRANSITION. When there is a product, the clip carries
  `view-transition-name: shot-<handle>`, paired with the same name on
  `product-gallery.liquid`, so the card's shot morphs into the product page's.
  The name is emitted ONLY with a product: this snippet renders many times per
  page, and a duplicated or empty name breaks the transition for the whole
  document — so a placeholder card must carry none.

  PLACEHOLDER MODE. Called with no `product`, every slot renders its labelled
  placeholder at final size: the photo becomes the design system's tone block,
  the material, name and price become Copy placeholders. That is what the
  catalogue draws until the store has products, and it is the same rule the
  content module already uses for unwritten copy — so the grid, its rhythm and
  its card proportions are the real ones before a single product exists.

  Accepts: product, sizes, heading_level ('h2' | 'h3')
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign heading_tag = heading_level | default: 'h3'
  assign new_label = 'product.new' | t
  assign sold_out_label = 'product.sold_out' | t
  assign card_sizes = sizes | default: '(min-width: 1024px) 23vw, (min-width: 640px) 45vw, 90vw'

  if product
    assign card_price = product.price
    assign card_compare = product.compare_at_price
    assign card_material = product.metafields.custom.material.value
    assign card_url = product.url
    assign card_image = product.featured_image

    assign discount = 0
    if card_compare and card_compare > card_price
      assign off = card_compare | minus: card_price | times: 100.0 | divided_by: card_compare
      assign discount = off | round
    endif

    assign is_new = false
    if product.tags contains 'New'
      assign is_new = true
    endif
  endif
-%}

<article class="group relative flex flex-col gap-3">
  <div class="relative">
    {%- comment -%}
      The clip lives on a static parent: scaling an element that clips itself
      would animate the crop along with the image. That static parent is also
      what carries the view-transition name — a morphing element must not be
      the one being scaled.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-sm"{% if product %} style="view-transition-name: shot-{{ product.handle }}"{% endif %}>
      {%- comment -%}
        `--duration-base` and the file's default interactive easing, matching the
        bag action below. `--duration-enter` with `--ease-entrance` is the scroll
        reveal curve — written for a still page, and on a pointer hover it reads
        as slow and floaty.

        NO PRESS STATE HERE, deliberately. This element already owns `transform`
        for the hover scale, and Android Chrome sets `:active` on touch-down
        before it knows the gesture is a scroll — a press scale would flash a
        shrink across the whole grid on every flick. Press feedback belongs on
        the two actions, which are real controls.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="transition-transform duration-(--duration-base) group-hover:scale-[1.03] group-has-focus-visible:scale-[1.03]">
        {% render 'media', image: card_image, sizes: card_sizes, ratio: '1 / 1' %}
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- if discount > 0 -%}
      {%- assign discount_label = '−' | append: discount | append: '%' -%}
      {% render 'badge', tone: 'sale', label: discount_label, class: 'absolute left-3 top-3' %}
    {%- elsif is_new -%}
      {% render 'badge', tone: 'invert', label: new_label, class: 'absolute left-3 top-3' %}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Above the stretched link, so both actions stay clickable. {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="absolute bottom-3 right-3 z-1 flex gap-2">
      {% render 'save-button', product: product %}

      <a
        href="{% if card_url %}{{ card_url }}#options{% else %}#{% endif %}"
        class="wl-tap inline-flex size-9.5 items-center justify-center rounded-xs bg-brand text-on-brand transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-invert"
        {% unless card_url %}aria-disabled="true" tabindex="-1"{% endunless %}
      >
        {% render 'icon', icon: 'bag', class: 'size-4.5' %}
        <span class="sr-only">{{ 'product.choose_size' | t }}{% if product %}: {{ product.title }}{% endif %}</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
    {%- comment -%}
      The material is a micro label, not a caption — the same treatment the
      product page gives the same field (`main-product.liquid`), so one field
      does not read two ways across two routes.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="text-micro uppercase tracking-label text-ink-subtle">
      {% render 'copy', value: card_material, label: 'material' %}
    </p>

    {%- comment -%}
      `text-balance` is set here rather than inherited: base.css balances `h1, h2`
      only, and this card's heading level is the caller's (`heading_level`), so
      without it the identical card wraps optically on the catalogue's `h2`s and
      greedily on the home grid's `h3`s.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <{{ heading_tag }} class="font-display text-h3 leading-h3 text-balance text-ink sm:text-card sm:leading-card">
      {%- if card_url -%}
        <a href="{{ card_url }}" class="after:absolute after:inset-0 after:content-[''] hover:text-brand">
          {% render 'copy', value: product.title, label: 'product name' %}
        </a>
      {%- else -%}
        {% render 'copy', value: '', label: 'product name' %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </{{ heading_tag }}>

    {% render 'price', price: card_price, compare_at: card_compare, class: 'mt-0.5' %}
  </div>

  {%- if product and product.available == false -%}
    {% render 'badge', tone: 'inert', label: sold_out_label, class: 'self-start' %}
  {%- endif -%}
</article>
